---
layout: article
title: Challenge automated traffic
description: Create Appwrite Firewall challenge rules that verify suspected bots before their requests continue.
difficulty: intermediate
readtime: 5
---

A challenge rule verifies matching clients before their requests continue. Clients that pass the challenge continue to your application. Clients that fail do not. Use challenge instead of deny when automated traffic and normal users share the same paths.

The interactive challenge page is served for **site** and **function** traffic when a browser navigates to the resource. **API**-scoped challenge rules reject matching requests with a `403` challenge-required error, because API clients cannot solve an interactive challenge. Scope challenge rules to a site or function when you expect browser traffic. See [Challenge](/docs/products/firewall/actions#challenge).

# Create the challenge rule {% #create-the-challenge-rule %}

{% only_dark %}
![Create firewall rule wizard with challenge settings](/images/docs/firewall/dark/challenge-bots.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create firewall rule wizard with challenge settings](/images/docs/firewall/challenge-bots.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. Open **Firewall** in your project.
2. Click **Create rule**.
3. Enter a **Rule name** (for example `Challenge automated clients`).
4. Select the **Resource type** for the traffic you expect. Pick **Sites** and select your site for browser traffic.
5. Under conditions, set **User agent** **Contains** `curl`.
6. Set **Action** to **Challenge**.
7. Keep **Difficulty** at `3`.
8. Keep **TTL (seconds)** at `1800`.
9. Click **Create rule**.

**Difficulty** sets the client-side proof-of-work cost, from `1` (easiest) to `5` (hardest). **TTL** sets how long a visitor stays cleared after a passed challenge.

# Cover more client patterns {% #cover-more-client-patterns %}

Conditions on one rule must all match. To challenge several user agent patterns, create one rule for each pattern. Common patterns include:

- **User agent** **Contains** `python-requests`
- **User agent** **Contains** `Go-http-client`
- **User agent** **Is empty**, for clients that send no user agent

Give the rules the same action and the same priority range so they behave as one policy.

# Verify the rule {% #verify-the-rule %}

1. Send a request with a matching user agent to the protected resource.
2. Confirm the client receives a challenge instead of the normal response.
3. Open **Firewall** and confirm the **Challenged** series rises in the traffic overview. The **Challenged** tile also reports solved challenges and the average solve time.

Raise the difficulty if automated traffic keeps passing. Lower it if normal users report slow page loads on first visit.

